Example 1 – adding unit fractions
  Calculate &frac{1}{3} + &frac{1}{4}
  LCM of 3 and 4 = 12
  &frac{1}{3} = &frac{4}{12} and &frac{1}{4} = &frac{3}{12}
  &frac{4}{12} + &frac{3}{12} = &frac{7}{12}
  = &frac{7}{12}
  (Convert both fractions to twelfths, then add numerators.)

Example 2 – subtracting fractions
  Calculate &frac{3}{4} - &frac{2}{5}
  LCM of 4 and 5 = 20
  &frac{3}{4} = &frac{15}{20} and &frac{2}{5} = &frac{8}{20}
  &frac{15}{20} - &frac{8}{20} = &frac{7}{20}
  = &frac{7}{20}
  (Same process: find common denominator, then subtract.)

Find a common denominator and add.

(a) &frac{1}{2} + &frac{1}{3}
(b) &frac{1}{4} + &frac{1}{5}
(c) &frac{1}{3} + &frac{1}{6}
(d) &frac{1}{5} + &frac{1}{10}

Add or subtract the mixed numbers.

(a) 1&frac{1}{2} + 2&frac{1}{3}
(b) 3&frac{1}{4} - 1&frac{1}{5}
(c) 2&frac{2}{3} + 1&frac{3}{4}
(d) 4&frac{1}{2} - 2&frac{2}{5}

Add or subtract. Simplify your answer.

(a) &frac{2}{5} + &frac{3}{10}
(b) &frac{7}{9} - &frac{1}{3}
(c) 1&frac{3}{8} + 2&frac{1}{6}
(d) &frac{5}{6} + &frac{2}{9} - &frac{1}{3}
(e) 3&frac{1}{3} - 1&frac{3}{4}

✓ &frac{1}{3} + &frac{1}{4} = &frac{7}{12}
✗ &frac{1}{3} + &frac{1}{4} = &frac{2}{7}
✓ To add fractions we need a common denominator
✗ &frac{3}{5} - &frac{1}{3} = &frac{2}{2} = 1
✓ &frac{5}{6} - &frac{1}{3} = &frac{5}{6} - &frac{2}{6} = &frac{3}{6} = &frac{1}{2}

